Building distribution packages
==============================

Debian / Ubuntu
---------------

debhelper expects debian/ at the top of the source tree, and this repo
keeps packaging out of the root so that a checkout looks like source
rather than like a package. So the directory is copied into place for a
build and removed afterwards:

    cp -r packaging/debian debian
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b
    rm -rf debian

Two packages come out: `uictl` (daemon, clients, units, AppArmor
profile) and `libuictl-dev` (header, static and shared library,
pkg-config file).

Fedora / RHEL
-------------

    VERSION=0.3.0
    git archive --format=tar.gz --prefix=uictl-$VERSION/ \
        -o ~/rpmbuild/SOURCES/uictl-$VERSION.tar.gz HEAD
    rpmbuild -ba packaging/uictl.spec


What the packages deliberately do NOT do
----------------------------------------

1. Add anyone to the `input` group. That membership is the privilege
   this whole project exists to concentrate in one binary, and a package
   that handed it out on install would be undoing its own purpose. The
   postinst says so and stops there.

2. Enable the socket unit. It is a *user* unit, and a package installing
   for the whole machine cannot meaningfully enable something per-user
   for people who are not logged in.

3. Enforce the AppArmor profile. It is loaded on install if AppArmor is
   running; switching a machine's confinement into enforce mode during a
   package install is the administrator's decision. Start it in complain
   mode and read the denials first -- see the header of
   apparmor/usr.libexec.uictld.

Neither packaging runs the device test suites at build time. They inject
into a real /dev/uinput and read the result back from
/dev/input/event*, which a build host does not have -- and a build that
typed into a machine it does not own would be a remarkable thing to
ship. The device-free suites do run: they check the specification
against the headers and the generated schema against both.
